  "narration": {
    "script": "How does a gas pump know when to stop? That click? A clever little mechanism inside the [[emph +]] nozzle just shut it off. See this tiny [[emph +]] hole near the tip? While you're pumping, the rushing fuel sucks [[emph +]] air in through it. But when gasoline rises high enough to cover the hole, that airflow [[emph +]] stops. Now the [[emph +]] suction pulls on a diaphragm, releasing a spring-loaded valve. And the gas stops flowing. All because of that tiny [[emph +]] hole.",
    "voice": {
      "profile": "hold-up-narrator"
    },
    "take": {
      "id": "rev4-recommended",
      "approvedBy": "Evan Wheeler",
